 ## Background
-The General Perspective Transformation was introduced in the context of hierarchical and parallelizable direct volume rendering for irregular and multiple grids [S1]. It addresses the specific challenges of volume rendering where correct depth ordering of many small primitives (like isosurface triangles) is critical for correct compositing [S1]. The method was developed to overcome the limitations of standard OpenGL-style perspective projections which assume the eye is at the origin or the projection plane is at $z=0$, leading to precision issues when the scene is far from the origin [S1].
+The method originates from work on hierarchical and parallelizable direct volume rendering for irregular and multiple grids [S1]. It was developed to address the limitations of standard perspective transformations where the projection plane passes through the origin, which was insufficient for maintaining precision in screen-z values for large visibility bounding boxes [S1]. The concept of perspective projection creating an illusion of depth is a fundamental principle in computer graphics, often implemented using functions like `glFrustum` in OpenGL-based tools like Smokeview [S2, unofficial].
 
